+# Privacy engineering and vendor review
+
+## Current data inventory
+
+| Surface | Data / purpose | Retention / recipient |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| HTTP delivery | IP address, request URL and browser headers reach the server to deliver public content | Transient in application memory; no application request logging or database |
+| Browser | Local HTML, CSS and coffee photo | No cookies, storage, identifiers, or analytics; responses use `no-store` |
+| Hosting / proxy | May see connection metadata and URLs | Deployment owner must establish provider, region, access controls, logging, and retention before release |
+| Unsplash | Source of the checked-in coffee image | Build-time download only; no visitor connection |
+
+No accounts, rewards, checkout, forms, or payment processing exist in this codebase. Navigation links to those routes predate this change and return 404. Do not send personal data in URLs. No user data is written to disk or exposed through errors. Only explicitly listed public files are served. The application has no runtime npm dependencies and no install-time remote server download.
+
+## Deployment requirements
+
+Run `npm start` with Node 22 or later (`PORT` defaults to 11222). Use this server rather than a generic static server to retain the response protections. Run `npm test` for regression coverage.
+
+Terminate TLS at a trusted ingress, redirect HTTP to HTTPS there, and prevent public access to the backend port. Set `HTTPS_ONLY=true` only after that configuration is active; this adds one-year HSTS. Do not enable includeSubDomains or preload without reviewing every subdomain. The server does not infer TLS from untrusted forwarding headers. If deploying the files on another host, configure the same headers at that host and recheck all routes and errors.
+
+Before production, the deployment owner must:
+
+- Identify the host and all subprocessors; document region, data access and retention. Disable access logging when unnecessary. If operational logs are needed, exclude query strings, cookies, authorization headers and full IP addresses; define a short, justified retention and enforce deletion.
+- Require MFA, least-privilege access and encryption for infrastructure records and backups. Test provider deletion and expiry. Keep secrets out of public assets and URLs.
+- Verify HTTPS redirects, certificates, HSTS, and header preservation at the public ingress. The local automated tests cannot verify hosting infrastructure.
+- Add a real operator contact channel to the privacy notice once one is supplied. Confirm the notice accurately reflects the actual hosting arrangements.
+
+## Third-party audit register
+
+This is a code-level inventory and review workflow, **not an independent audit or certification**. No third-party auditor has reviewed this deployment.
+
+| Component | Review finding | Follow-up |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Unsplash photo | Previously loaded remotely, revealing visitor connection metadata. Now served from `/assets/coffee.jpg`. Source: https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1495474472287-4d71bcdd2085 | Confirm image usage terms before publishing; retain source attribution here |
+| `pnpm dlx statikk` | Previously downloaded a server dynamically at startup | Removed; native Node HTTP server used instead |
+| Node runtime | Sole runtime dependency | Deployment owner keeps supported Node release patched |
+| Hosting / CDN / proxy | Not specified in repository | Vendor approval and infrastructure review pending before production |
+| Analytics, ads, fonts, payment SDKs | None present | Re-review before any introduction |
+
+For each new provider, record an owner, review date, purpose, exact fields disclosed, necessity, region, subprocessors, retention/deletion behavior, security evidence, incident notification terms, and approval. Prefer an integration that shares no visitor data. Obtain an independent assessment of actual production infrastructure before describing the application as externally audited. Review providers before release, after changes, and at least annually.
+
+## Future data features
+
+Before implementing account or order services, define required fields and retention for each purpose. Separate optional marketing consent, keep it disabled initially, and honor withdrawal. Validate requests server-side, restrict access by role, encrypt transport and stored sensitive data, use secure HttpOnly SameSite session cookies, and provide verified access/export/deletion flows. Never store payment card details in this application. Update both this inventory and the public notice before collecting anything new.
+
+## Header references
+
+The restrictive CSP is delivered as an HTTP header, including `frame-ancestors` (which is not supported in a meta policy). External styles and images are limited to the same origin; scripts and connections are disabled.
+
+- https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Reference/Headers/Content-Security-Policy/frame-ancestors
+- https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Reference/Headers/Strict-Transport-Security
